\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Foreign Reserves May Rise By About $2.7B In Jan  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Terry Wade  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2004\par 16:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's foreign reserves likely rose sharply in January as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively bought dollars to insulate against future economic shocks.  \par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse First Boston and the Tendencias consultancy in Sao Paulo on Thursday estimated January purchases, respectively, at $2.6 billion and $2.7 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't detail its purchases for the month until Feb. 20, but its release Wednesday of flows in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for January allowed economists to deduce the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the month.  \par \par \uc2 Some $3.3 billion entered \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January. And despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar purchases, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, strengthened 1.3% in January until fears of potential interest rate hikes in the U.S. weakened the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. The real's strength for most of the month occurred as commercial banks sold about $700 million more on the local spot market than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , combined with a $1.5 billion sovereign bond issued in January, should boost net foreign reserves to $24.5 billion from $20.5 billion in December, CSFB said in a Thursday note.  \par \par \uc2 If the country continues to post strong trade surpluses and international liquidity remains heavy, CSFB said reserves could end the year between $30 billion and $35 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Still, Brazil's net foreign reserves, excluding some $30 billion in cash from the International Monetary Fund, would remain remarkably low and leave the real vulnerable to external and internal economic shocks.  \par \par \uc2 Net reserves fell sharply in 1998 as the country tried to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a balance of payments crisis before floating the real in early 1999. During the turmoil, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  burned through much of the country's net $70 billion in reserves.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles has said Brazil wants to go it alone in the world economy and leave the IMF when its current accord expires late this year.  \par \par \uc2 To do that, reserves need to rise sharply. Compared with its emerging market peers, Brazil's reserves are woefully inadequate. China has $450 billion in reserves and Taiwan $100 million. Mexico also has substantial foreign reserves, a key factor that allowed it to reach investment grade status, unlike Brazil.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1479; terry.wade@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040205e025000o3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
