\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Hits 3-Month High on Bond Buyback Talk  \b0\par\par\uc2 326 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 21:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's real rallied to its best finish since mid-October on talk about a possible Brazilian government bond buyback and dollar inflows from foreign bond sales.  \par \par \uc2 The real closed at 2.833 to the dollar, compared with 2.853 on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The rally happened even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil bought dollars in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at a rate of BRL2.844/dollar. Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adopted a new policy this week of buying dollars in order to boost its international reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The bank bought dollars at a rate of BRL2.862/dollar on Thursday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't say how much it purchased in either of the market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark C-bond, or Capitalization bond, rose above par for the first time. The bond is one of a series of Brady bonds issued in 1994 as the country came out of a debt restructuring. About $16 billion Brady bonds, $6.5 billion of them C-bonds, are outstanding.  \par \par \uc2 The C-bond's rally sparked speculation among investors that Brazil might buy back billions of dollars of the bonds using call options on April 15, the date of the next coupon payment.  \par \par \uc2 Economists expect Brazil to start a debt exchange in the near future, whether or not the government decides to use the call option.  \par \par \uc2 Also boosting the real were inflows from the sale of hundreds of millions of dollars of bonds by Brazilian companies abroad over the last few weeks.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday a unit of CVRD, the world's biggest iron ore exporter, sold $500 million of 30-year bonds, $200 million more than it had planned, thanks to strong investor demand. The issue is one of the longest-dated Brazilian corporate bonds ever sold.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Tom Vogel, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1480; tom.vogel@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040109e019000qd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
