\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Targets  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Reserves, Debt Buyback, IMF Freedom  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Terry Wade  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2004\par 20:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazilian monetary authorities have adopted an ambitious strategy to replenish foreign reserves that could lead to a large debt buyback in April and eventually free the country from reliance on the International Monetary Fund.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles said late Tuesday the bank will start purchasing dollars on the spot market and directly deposit them into international reserves. Meirelles said no target exists for the amount of purchases, but big buys are likely if dollar inflows remain strong.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury, for example, bought $11.2 billion on the spot market in 2003, much more than expected, to meet overseas debt payments extending into June of 2004. Unlike those of the Treasury, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's purchases will be transparent, with announcements of auctions and their results.  \par \par \uc2 Though some acknowledged the new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy might imply \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange-rate regime is a "dirty" instead of "clean" float, or that fewer interest rate cuts are likely as a weaker real will add to inflation, analysts reacted positively overall to the change.  \par \par \uc2 "The new policy of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is wise because it'll use real strength to rebuild foreign reserves and reduce reliance on the IMF," said Roger Scher, Latin American sovereign ratings director with Fitch in New York. "Ultimately, Brazil would like to leave the IMF program."  \par \par \uc2 In December, Brazil signed a $14 billion, one-year IMF deal when the government, after considerable internal debate, decided potential economic shocks were still too threatening to operate without a security blanket from the multilateral lender. Excluding some $30 billion in IMF funds, Brazil's net foreign reserves are only about $17 billion. The country had $70 billion in reserves in 1998 before it unsuccesfully tried to defend the real, then pegged to the dollar, from speculative attack.  \par \par \uc2 Octavio de Barros, chief economist for Banco Bradesco in Sao Paulo, thinks the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's increased emphasis on foreign reserves will direct debt issuance plans.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in my view, is giving a signal about the possibility of a debt buyback," he said. "Getting rid of Brady bonds would really help improve Brazil's image, because these bonds were sold as the country was coming out of a moratorium."  \par \par \uc2 Barros said Brazil might issue new bonds in the first quarter to refinance a host of Brady bonds. A buyback would eliminate millions of dollars in interest costs and reduce pressure on foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil has about $6.5 billion in outstanding Brady bonds, with an option to buy them back at face value on April 15, the next interest payment date. Making a buyback more likely, Brazil's closely-watched C-bond is trading nearly at par, at 99.563 bid. Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment.  \par \par \uc2 Besides debt policy, analysts agree a slightly weaker real in the near term will create conditions for a stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the long term by improving Brazil's external accounts. Strong external accounts will attract foreign investment levels and reduce borrowing costs.  \par \par \uc2 "If there is any goal here it is to run a trade surplus of more than $20 billion and keep the balance of payments healthy," while hitting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 5.5% inflation target for this year, said Barros.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil posted a record $25 billion trade surplus in 2003 and a current account surplus for the first time in a decade. Exporters say an exchange rate of BRL3.00 to the dollar will allow continued growth in foreign sales. Bradesco forecasts an average real price of BRL3.03 this year.  \par \par \uc2 The first day after the policy change, Brazil's real strengthened 0.3% to close at BRL2.858 per dollar Wednesday, with little sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases had undermined a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that gained 22% in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 "I'd have expected weakness today. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to let this sink in," said Yianos Kontopoulos, chief global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Merrill Lynch in New York. "We'll probably have to wait a few more days to see the full impacts."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1479; terry.wade@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040107e017000qz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
