\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Tsy Secy Denies Broader  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Terry Wade  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2003\par 16:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazilian Treasury Secretary Joaquim Levy on Monday denied the government has adopted a broader \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy to impact the value of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The government has bought $7.8 billion on the local spot market so far this year, considerably more than the guidance of $5.7 billion provided in October.  \par \par \uc2 Among other items, the extra purchases of more than $2.0 billion were made to pay back multilateral loans, buy drugs for the health ministry and obtain supplies for the army, according to a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official.  \par \par \uc2 Originally, the Treasury indicated the purchases would be made solely to service sovereign bonds.  \par \par \uc2 Explaining the larger than expected purchases, Levy said: "Sometimes there's a concentration of debt payments like we just had."  \par \par \uc2 He added that purchasing dollars for the army and health ministry doesn't indicate a shift in policy.  \par \par \uc2 "This is normal. Sometimes you have demands from specific sectors (of the government)," he said after speaking at a capital markets seminar here.  \par \par \uc2 He reiterated that "in a floating exchange-rate environment, everybody has the obligation to go to the market and buy and sell. This is true for both the Treasury and the private sector."  \par \par \uc2 Despite Levy's comments, the higher than expected purchases follow a November \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision that extended the settlement period for the Treasury's dollar purchases on the spot market to six months from about three. That substantially boosted the ability of the government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 Still, President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva's administration claims it has no targeted \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, as adopting one would contradict the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation-targeting program, which was put in place just after the real was allowed to float freely in early 1999.  \par \par \uc2 Officials also refuse to accept the word \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when discussing the dollar purchases. Yet the real, currently at about three per dollar, only changed 1.4% in value between June 1 and Dec. 1, suggesting to many the government has a preferred \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate.  \par \par \uc2 Besides collecting funds for debt payments abroad, the dollar purchases effectively meet other objectives.  \par \par \uc2 The government wants a relatively cheap real to maintain Brazil's export boom and deepen import substitution. Those trends created a surging trade surplus in recent quarters that may close the country's current account gap this year for the first time in a decade.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, the aggressive dollar purchases diminish pressure on the country's scant net foreign reserves. They are woefully low and need to rise before the country can convince all investors it can function without the security blanket of an accord with the International Monetary Fund.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's net reserves, excluding some $30 billion in IMF funds, total only about $18 billion. This compares with some $70 billion in reserves just before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started burning through dollars to fend off a balance of payments crisis in 1998, and $35 billion just after the real floated.  \par \par \uc2 Regarding dollar purchases in the future, Levy didn't explicitly back comments made in October that the Treasury would purchase $5.0 billion next year to make good on overseas debt payments.  \par \par \uc2 "Our purchases will be proportional to our obligations," he said.  \par \par \uc2 On other matters, Levy said market conditions are "favorable" for Brazil to issue sovereign debt but declined to provide specifics on when the government would start selling bonds to fund its $4.0 billion borrowing program for 2004.  \par \par \uc2 He also said the country's economic recovery remains solid and that the economy should expand by at least 3.0% to 3.5% next year. Levy said official data released Monday that showed industrial output fell 0.5% in October was expected after September's stellar result.  \par \par \uc2 -Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1479; terry.wade@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031208dzc8000mw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
