\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Opens Weaker, Market Expects  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2003\par 13:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real opened weaker Thursday amid expectations that the Treasury will enter the market after the government loosened rules for buying dollars on the spot market earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, news of a coming $14 billion extension to Brazil's accord with the International Monetary Fund had largely been priced in, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at BRL2.870 per dollar, compared with Tuesday's BRL2.862 close.  \par \par \uc2 Though traders said that the Treasury hasn't started buying in noticeable volume yet, the market is expecting Banco do Brasil to start buying cheap dollars on behalf of the Treasury in order to bolster reserves and pay down foreign debt.  \par \par \uc2 Fueling the expectations, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday extended the settlement period for spot dollar purchases by the Treasury from three to six months, boosting the government's ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 The real is widely predicted to weaken to BRL3.00 a dollar by the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Still, expectations of fresh dollar inflows have been keeping the real afloat, especially after Brazilian officials said this week they are planning to sign a new loan with the IMF. The accord will pad the country's low foreign reserves and should boost investor confidence in Brazil, paving the way for the private sector to issue new debt abroad.  \par \par \uc2 The market is waiting for money to come in from bonds sold recently by a slew of companies including Usiminas, Cosipa and Braskem, and traders said they expect more debt-deal announcements in coming days, keeping dollars flowing in to support the real.  \par \par \uc2 -By Hannah Karp, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1479; brazil@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1156 1843  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031106dzb6000pc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
