\par\par \b \uc2 Once A Dirty Word In  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 ,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Becomes Vogue  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Terry Wade  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2003\par 23:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Long the dirtiest word in Brazilian economics, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made a startling comeback in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 In investment circles, it's now as acceptable as President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva, a once-feared left-wing rabble-rouser who has become a market-friendly darling since taking office in January.  \par \par \uc2 It's in vogue even though Brazilians still vilify the man responsible for the country's biggest-ever \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Gustavo Franco, the central banker who spent $40 billion in foreign reserves in late 1998 and 1999 unsuccessfully trying to defend the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which at the time was pegged to the dollar - from speculative attack.  \par \par \uc2 So far this year, the Treasury has bought nearly $5.0 billion on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, from strengthening too much amid strong dollar inflows. Some $700 million in purchases are slated through December, along with another $5.0 billion next year.  \par \par \uc2 Though the government refuses to call them \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , critics of the purchases are hard to find.  \par \par \uc2 "What the government has been doing is extremely prudent," said Carlos Thadeu de Freitas, a professor at the Ibmec business school in Rio de Janeiro and former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  director.  \par \par \uc2 Lula's administration claims it has no targeted \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, as adopting one would contradict - and impede - the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation-targeting program, which was put in place just after the real was allowed to float freely in early 1999.  \par \par \uc2 But other priorities are in play. The government wants a relatively cheap real to maintain Brazil's export boom and deepen import substitution. Those trends created a surging trade surplus in recent quarters that may close the country's current account gap this year for the first time in a decade.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, the aggressive dollar purchases diminish pressure on the country's scant net foreign reserves. They are woefully low and need to rise before the country can convince all investors it can function without the security blanket of an accord with the International Monetary Fund. The government will decide this month if it will renegotiate an IMF package slated to expire later this year.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's net reserves, excluding some $30 billion in IMF funds, total only about $18 billion. This compares with some $70 billion in reserves just before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started burning through dollars to fend off a balance of payments crisis in 1998, and $35 billion just after the real floated. The Treasury hopes to boost net reserves next year to about $20 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury Secretary Joaquim Levy said Wednesday the recent dollar purchases don't amount to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It's not our intent (to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), that's why we're buying dollars in the most homogenous way possible. The Treasury doesn't play the market: it goes up and down," he said Wednesday in Brasilia.  \par \par \uc2 A closer look, however, shows traders are correct when they say BRL2.90 per dollar is the level the government is targeting. It has barely moved from that rate for the last four months.  \par \par \uc2 Between June 1 and Oct. 1, the real stayed in an unusually tight trading range and moved just 1.5% - an extremely rare feat for one of the world's most volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Wednesday it closed at BRL2.905.  \par \par \uc2 Though he disavowed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Levy suggested the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices are changing and no longer restricted by the official policy of the last four years: that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only occur when liquidity problems arise. Levy has yet to detail the size and frequency of the "homogenous" purchases, but more data are expected this week.  \par \par \uc2 "Increasingly, in a floating exchange-rate environment, everyone has their own obligation to go to the market and buy and sell. This is true for the private sector and the Treasury," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury officials weren't available to further comment on Levy's statements, which didn't impact a real that's pricing in $5.0 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  next year.  \par \par \uc2 -By Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-8147-0853; terry.wade@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031001dza1000xb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
