\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:Brazil Meirelles:Curr Acct May Be Positive In '03  \b0\par\par\uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2003\par 19:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds analysis, analyst comments and background)  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Terry Wade \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Henrique Meirelles Monday said Brazil may post a current account surplus in 2003, a result that would prove the country made the right decision in 1999 to float its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to escape from a suffocating external financing gap.  \par \par \uc2 "With all the adjustments we've been making, our current account deficit has been on a downward trend," Meirelles said at a conference in Rio de Janeiro.  \par \par \uc2 "Our estimate of a $1.2 billion deficit (for 2003) is conservative. There's a strong possibility that we'll have a positive result in 2003," he added.  \par \par \uc2 A swelling trade surplus, expected to hit about $20 billion this year, is helping to produce current account surpluses on a monthly basis. August's surplus was $1.2 billion.  \par \par \uc2 If the current account for the year ends up positive, it would mark the first time since 1993, just before the country essentially pegged its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar and a balance of payments shortfall began to worry investors. In 1998, just before Brazil floated its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the current account deficit was $33.5 billion.  \par \par \uc2 With a cheap \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deepening Brazil's external orientation by encouraging exports and import substitution, the trade surplus has grown sharply in recent quarters - and at just the right time - to plug up the current account gap.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil relied heavily on foreign direct investment and privatization proceeds to fund its current account gap after floating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Rising trade surpluses are now compensating for falling foreign direct investment, which is expected to total about $9.0 billion this year, down from more than $30 billion only three years ago.  \par \par \uc2 When combined with international credit lines that have been restored since being cut in 2002 on the eve of presidential elections, monthly current account surpluses have helped boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  22% this year.  \par \par \uc2 And this historic turnaround in Brazil's external accounts would have had a more pronounced effect on the real if the Treasury weren't indirectly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.  \par \par \uc2 "The appreciation in the exchange rate in recent months as a result of the good external flows would have been much bigger without the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Treasury," Octavio de Barros, chief economist at Banco Bradesco, said Monday in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's government has been pursuing an aggressive two-pronged strategy to preserve foreign reserves and prevent the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too much.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday said the Treasury has bought $4.95 billion on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since January. The money is being used to pay off the government's external debts. Another $700 million in purchases will be made through December. And next year, some $5.0 billion in purchases are planned.  \par \par \uc2 Though the government says its dollar purchases don't amount to an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, traders disagree, as \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exporters say the country's rising trade balance would be hurt by a real stronger than BRL2.90 to the dollar. The real was quoted at BRL2.9350 to the dollar midafternoon Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The purchases, funded by reals, diminish pressure on the country's scant net foreign reserves, which are expected to total about $16.9 billion at year-end. Including International Monetary Fund money, Brazil will end the year with about $46.2 billion in foreign reserves. In 2004, net foreign reserves are expected to climb to $20 billion and gross reserves to ease to $43.7 billion.  \par \par \uc2 -Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1479; terry.wade@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Adriana Brasileiro in Rio de Janeiro contributed to this report)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030929dz9t000t6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
