\par\par \b \uc2 ODJ UPDATE:Brazil Meirelles:Curr Acct May Be Positive In '03  \b0\par\par\uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2003\par 20:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par (Adds analysis, analyst comments and background) \par  \par  \par    By Terry Wade \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \par SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Henrique Meirelles Monday said \par Brazil may post a current account surplus in 2003, a result that would prove \par the country made the right decision in 1999 to float its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to escape \par from a suffocating external financing gap. \par  \par "With all the adjustments we've been making, our current account deficit has \par been on a downward trend," Meirelles said at a conference in Rio de Janeiro. \par  \par "Our estimate of a $1.2 billion deficit (for 2003) is conservative. There's a \par strong possibility that we'll have a positive result in 2003," he added. \par  \par A swelling trade surplus, expected to hit about $20 billion this year, is \par helping to produce current account surpluses on a monthly basis. August's \par surplus was $1.2 billion. \par  \par If the current account for the year ends up positive, it would mark the first \par time since 1993, just before the country essentially pegged its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \par the dollar and a balance of payments shortfall began to worry investors. In \par 1998, just before Brazil floated its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the current account deficit was \par $33.5 billion. \par  \par With a cheap \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deepening Brazil's external orientation by encouraging \par exports and import substitution, the trade surplus has grown sharply in recent \par quarters - and at just the right time - to plug up the current account gap. \par  \par Brazil relied heavily on foreign direct investment and privatization proceeds \par to fund its current account gap after floating its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Rising trade \par surpluses are now compensating for falling foreign direct investment, which is \par expected to total about $9.0 billion this year, down from more than $30 \par billion only three years ago. \par  \par When combined with international credit lines that have been restored since \par being cut in 2002 on the eve of presidential elections, monthly current \par account surpluses have helped boost the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  22% this year. \par  \par And this historic turnaround in Brazil's external accounts would have had a \par more pronounced effect on the real if the Treasury weren't indirectly \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market. \par  \par "The appreciation in the exchange rate in recent months as a result of the \par good external flows would have been much bigger without the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \par the Treasury," Octavio de Barros, chief economist at Banco Bradesco, said \par Monday in a research note. \par  \par Brazil's government has been pursuing an aggressive two-pronged strategy to \par preserve foreign reserves and prevent the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from strengthening \par too much. \par  \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday said the Treasury has bought $4.95 billion on the \par local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market since January. The money is being used to pay \par off the government's external debts. Another $700 million in purchases will be \par made through December. And next year, some $5.0 billion in purchases are \par planned. \par  \par Though the government says its dollar purchases don't amount to an \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy, traders disagree, as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's exporters say the \par country's rising trade balance would be hurt by a real stronger than BRL2.90 \par to the dollar. The real was quoted at BRL2.9350 to the dollar midafternoon \par Monday. \par  \par The purchases, funded by reals, diminish pressure on the country's scant net \par foreign reserves, which are expected to total about $16.9 billion at year-end. \par Including International Monetary Fund money, Brazil will end the year with \par about $46.2 billion in foreign reserves. In 2004, net foreign reserves are \par expected to climb to $20 billion and gross reserves to ease to $43.7 billion. \par  \par -Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1479; terry.wade@dowjones.com \par  \par (Adriana Brasileiro in Rio de Janeiro contributed to this report) \par Copyright 2003 OsterDowJones Commodity News (ODJ). All rights reserved. \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCS000020030929dz9t000hu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
