\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Ends Stronger At BRL2.902 To The Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 234 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 22:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's real ended stronger Thursday, as heavy dollar inflows prevented the real from slipping on an interest-rate cut made late Wednesday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at BRL2.902 to the dollar, compared with Wednesday's BRL2.911 finish.  \par \par \uc2 The inflows supported the real after a 2.0 percentage point interest-rate cut to 20% a year for the benchmark rate. Traders said the cut was largely priced in.  \par \par \uc2 The lower base rate should help revive a stalled economy in the coming months and that's helping attract dollar inflows for Brazilian assets like stocks.  \par \par \uc2 But the bulk of inflows are coming from corporate recent debt sales overseas. Among the larger deals, drinks giant AmBev sold $500 million overseas last week, while oil giant Petrobras placed $250 million.  \par \par \uc2 Traders continue to doubt the real can hold onto levels stronger than BRL2.90, a point where dollar-buying by government entities has occurred in recent weeks. State-run Banco do Brasil has routinely been buying dollars for the Treasury to make payments on external debts.  \par \par \uc2 Traders interpret this as a way of indirectly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real cheap enough to ensure continued strong exports.  \par \par \uc2 -By Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1479; terry.wade@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1156 1843  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030918dz9i00172\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\png