\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil stocks slump on phone spat,  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  firms. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Todd Benson \par \uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2003\par 23:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Brazil, June 27 (Reuters) - Brazilian stocks tumbled on Friday as the government butted heads with telephone companies over rate hikes, but the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed amid a heavy inflow of dollars. \par \par \uc2  Tracking Wall Street's sharp decline, the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange's benchmark Bovespa index fell 0.67 percent to 13,024.1 points, marking a 0.8 percent drop for the week. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's new left-leaning government said it was studying ways to block an increase in phone rates authorized late on Thursday by the National Telecommunications Agency, the sector regulator known as Anatel. \par \par \uc2  The new tariff guidelines allow fixed-line operators to raise residential rates by between 24.5 percent and 25 percent, while authorizing a 41.7 percent hike on business lines, public phones and installation fees. \par \par \uc2  Though the new fees are expected to significantly boost revenues for phone companies, investors were worried about the brewing spat between the government and Anatel. \par \par \uc2  "The situation caused a lot of unease in the market," said Helio Ozaki, a financial analyst at Finambras brokerage in Sao Paulo. "It just highlights the fragility of the regulatory system in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  and how vulnerable these agencies are to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The dispute sent telecommunications shares tumbling, led by Brasil Telecom and market bellwether Tele Norte Leste Participacoes, both of which were allowed to raise rates by 25 percent. \par \par \uc2  Stock in Brasil Telecom skidded 2.5 percent to 21.45 reais, while Telemar shares - which account for 14 percent of the Bovespa index - slipped 0.6 percent to 33.10 reais. \par \par \uc2  Shares in the Brazilian unit of Spain's Telefonica, which can hike rates by 24.5 percent, fell 0.27 percent to 33.80 reais. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, however, largely ignored the row. The Brazilian real firmed 0.7 percent to 2.88 per dollar, finishing the week a half-percentage point stronger. \par \par \uc2  The real, which has rallied 23 percent against the greenback so far this year, crept higher as fresh cash flowed into the market following a slew of recent bond issues by Brazilian companies abroad, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "With all the cash companies are raising abroad right now, it's hard to bet against the real," said Hideaki Iha, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Souza Barros brokerage in Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  "Not even the debt auction was enough to stress out the market," Iha added, referring to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's attempt to roll over $700 million in dollar-linked debt. It ended up only selling $335.7 million. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030627dz6r0124a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
