\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Lacks Interest In Argentine  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Idea \b0\par\par\uc2  By Terry Wade and Gerald Jeffris \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 2003\par 23:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- Argentina wants to adopt a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  targeting system with its northern neighbor at a time when Brazilian officials indicate that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a dirty word. \par \par \uc2  That means a plan Argentine Vice Foreign Minister Martin Redrado presented Monday in Brasilia that would aim to keep Brazil's real and Argentina's peso around three per dollar may be rejected unless it's revised. \par \par \uc2  Though the blueprint would mark the strongest effort yet to integrate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Common Market of the South, or Mercosur, trade bloc adopted it as a medium-term goal in the late 1990s, it likely won't be put into practice for a long time. Mercosur includes Brazil, Argentina, Uruguay and Paraguay. \par \par \uc2  "There's no timeframe for implementation," Redrado said in Brasilia. He suggested attaining near parity between the peso and real by adopting trading bands or increasing the use of reciprocal credits. "This would be a transitional system to a common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Henrique Meirelles and Finance Minister Antonio Palocci, the top economic policy-makers in President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva's center-left administration, have said repeatedly in the last month that they won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or adopt \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  targets. \par \par \uc2  Monday, Meirelles and Palocci tried to distance themselves from Redrado's approach. \par \par \uc2  Meirelles said he was unaware of any debate on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bands. Palocci met Redrado and said they didn't discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  bands but agreed to study "macroeconomic policy convergence" between the two countries. \par \par \uc2  There are a host of stumbling blocks to Redrado's plans. Mercosur leaders have never said how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for an integrated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be composed, and heavyweight \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  would prefer to create \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  integration without using \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Redrado's scheme also assumes a win of Argentina's presidential election May 18 by government-backed candidate Nestor Kirchner over former President Carlos Menem, who opposes Mercosur \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  integration and strongly favors closer ties with the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Redrado said the creation of a band for the peso and real would give more certainty to exporters, importers and investors. On the trade front, Redrado indicated Brazil will offer increased export financing to Argentina. \par \par \uc2  Though \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness rattled their economies for much of 2002, worries of excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength have recently jumped to the forefront of economic policy debates in Argentina and Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Since January, the peso and real have each gained about 20% against the dollar - prompting fears that surging exports and increased import substitution may slow to the  detriment of the countries' best performing sectors. \par \par \uc2  The peso hit its highest levels in more than a year last week and Monday closed at ARS2.80 a dollar, while the real finished at BRL3.04 and is hovering around its strongest levels since August. \par \par \uc2  Top aides of Lula, who is still trying to cultivate a pro-market image after decades of advocating policies that frighten investors, say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is a tricky game that's not worthwhile. \par \par \uc2  But Lula's planning and trade ministers have advocated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure exports keep growing and the current account gap continues narrowing. \par \par \uc2  Lula himself last week suggested too much real strength was bad. But that surprised investors and he quickly backtracked. \par \par \uc2  "The real will continue to fluctuate and the government won't interfere with it," he said Friday. \par \par \uc2  -Terry Wade and Gerald Jeffris, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.9117.0969; brazil@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Michael Casey in Buenos Aires contributed to this report) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030505dz550014w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
