\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Real Opens At BRL2.900/Dlr Vs BRL2.912 End Wed \b0\par\par\uc2 309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2003\par 15:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  RIO DE JANEIRO -(Dow Jones)- Brazil's real opened slightly stronger against the dollar Friday as investors are optimistic the success of the country's $1 billion sovereign bond last week will lead local corporate borrowers to tap capital markets in the short term. \par \par \uc2  The real opened at BRL2.900 per dollar compared with Wednesday's closing mark of BRL2.912 to the dollar. Local markets were closed Thursday for Labor Day. \par \par \uc2  By 1320 GMT, the real was trading at BRL2.904 to the dollar, but traders cautioned that volume was lower-than-average and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely remain stable. \par \par \uc2  Traders said investors overlooked comments by President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva that the government doesn't want the real to rally too much because it may hurt exports. \par \par \uc2  Brazilian media reported Friday the president made the comments during Labor Day celebrations in Sao Paulo, Brazil's financial center. \par \par \uc2  "The real didn't really react to Lula's comments, but liquidity is very thin today, so it's hard to judge," said a trader in Sao Paulo. "Once the market digests his comments and its possible implications, the real may move lower," he added. \par \par \uc2  Some market watchers believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take steps to rein in the real's rally to ensure export growth. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry have repeatedly denied the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lauded \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's free \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  Still, export growth is key for Brazil's trade surplus, which is seen hitting $16 billion this year, and for a smaller current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, a stronger real spells easing inflation, which is seen topping 12% in 2003. \par \par \uc2  -By Adriana Brasileiro, Dow Jones Newswires; (5521) 3288-5004; adriana.brasileiro@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030502dz52000sn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
