\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Petrobras To Unveil Fuel Price Adjustment Fri \b0\par\par\uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2002\par 16:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  RIO DE JANEIRO -(Dow Jones)- Brazil's oil giant Petroleo Brasileiro (PBR), or Petrobras, Friday unveiled a 12.09% raise in gasoline prices at the refinery level effective Nov. 4. \par \par \uc2  Petrobras said in a statement it's also hiking diesel fuel prices by 20.5%, effective Nov. 4, and the price of liquefied petroleum gas, known as cooking gas, by 22.8%, effective Nov. 5. \par \par \uc2  This comes four months after Petrobras made the last adjustment to gasoline prices. \par \par \uc2  The company said in a statement it opted to wait for the  volatility of the last few months to ease before making a decision on fuel prices. \par \par \uc2  "Today, with most of the uncertainties out of the way, international oil prices and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  variations are  at higher levels than those registered by the company when local fuel prices were last adjusted," Petrobras said in the statement. \par \par \uc2  Petrobras said the impact of the wholesale price hikes at the pump will likely be 9% on average for gasoline, 17% for diesel fuel and 12% for cooking gas. \par \par \uc2  The state-owned company, which had its hands tied with government regulation for the past two months, was expected to hike fuel prices right after the Oct. 27 runoff election in which left-leaning Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva won a landslide victory against the government-backed candidate Jose Serra. \par \par \uc2  Market watchers said the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Petrobras' free-market pricing policy - effective since January this year - was a politically motivated attempt to boost Serra's chances in the polls. \par \par \uc2  Analysts estimated it would be necessary for Petrobras to raise gasoline prices by around 20% to make up for higher oil prices abroad and the sharp depreciation of the real in past months. While predicting adjustments would come in installments to ease the impact  on inflation, many estimated a hike of around 10% by early November, with the rest to be administered by the next government, which takes office Jan. 1. \par \par \uc2  Economists fear gas price hikes will fuel Brazil's recent surge in inflation, which looks likely to close at  8% this year - well above the government's 1.5%-5.5% target band for this year and 4% goal for 2003. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said gasoline prices for consumers would likely increase by 9% by the end of the year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  also predicted a further 9% increase in gasoline prices in 2003. \par \par \uc2  Gasoline prices had been unchanged since June 30, when Petrobras announced a 6.75% hike. Petrobras hiked diesel prices by 9.5% on the same date. \par \par \uc2  Since then the Brazilian real ($1=BRL3.63) has lost about 22%, while international oil prices abroad have climbed on fears of conflict in the Middle East. \par \par \uc2  Local prices are heavily affected by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  variations  and international prices. Even though Petrobras exports oil, it must import about a quarter of the 1.8 million barrels that Brazil consumes daily as the country lacks refining capacity. Petrobras also imports about 40% of the natural gas used in the country. \par \par \uc2  In mid-August President Fernando Henrique Cardoso ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's oil sector watchdog to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in fuel prices, effectively returning to a policy of regulation that was to have ended Jan. 1. \par \par \uc2  Prior to that date, Petrobras held a monopoly on oil product imports and prices were regulated by the government. \par \par \uc2  -By Adriana Brasileiro, Dow Jones Newswires; (5521) 3288-5004, adriana.brasileiro@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021101dyb1000wt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
