\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Brazil's president-elect hails democratic transfer. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Axel Bugge \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2002\par 21:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRASILIA, Brazil, Oct 29 (Reuters) - Brazil's President-elect Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva hailed the outgoing president on Tuesday for initiating an unprecedented transition of power which could prove key to the success of the country's first elected left-wing administration. \par \par \uc2  Kicking off the transition of power after his watershed victory in a presidential election runoff on Sunday, Lula met with President Fernando Henrique Cardoso to ensure a smooth handover at a time of financial turmoil. \par \par \uc2  After the meeting, Lula, as he is known universally in Brazil, announced that his Workers' Party's government program coordinator Antonio Palocci would head a team to establish the new administration ahead of the January takeover. \par \par \uc2  "I think the moment we are living has to be unique in Latin America's political history," Lula said in his first statement from the presidential palace since winning the elections. \par \par \uc2  "And who knows if this lesson will not remain forever for those of us who have seen ministers and rulers leave by the back door in order not to receive the elected one," said the former union leader. \par \par \uc2  Lula's warm thanks to Cardoso for helping the transition underlined the significance of this transfer of power in a country whose turbulent political history is pot-marked with military \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and unfinished presidencies. \par \par \uc2  Since \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's return to democracy in 1985 after two decades of dictatorship, only one president - Cardoso - has seen out his rule normally. One died, while another resigned when faced with possible impeachment amid a graft scandal. \par \par \uc2  This will be only the second time in the history of Latin America's largest country that one elected president hands over the presidential sash to another. \par \par \uc2  A smooth transition from Cardoso's eight-year rule is also crucial to ensure that Brazil's delicate economy returns to robust growth after months of turmoil in financial markets over the prospect of a left turn in the country. \par \par \uc2  Markets were eagerly expecting the announcement of more members of Lula's transition team, in the hope of learning more about his government's likely economic policies. \par \par \uc2  But stocks and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were higher after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to sell $951.5 million in dollar-linked domestic debt just a week after the bank was unable to roll over debts. \par \par \uc2  Palocci said he would have his full transition team ready in two days, even though there was no guarantee that the members of the team would become ministers in Lula's cabinet. \par \par \uc2  "Palocci was always going to be part of Lula's command team," said Sandra Utsumi, chief economist at BES Investimentos in Sao Paulo. "There is a big expectation about potentials for the ministries and as there are no names, as they are delayed for two days, that creates a climate of apprehension." \par \par \uc2  GAPING SOCIAL DIVIDE \par \par \uc2  Lula has promised to calm financial jitters in the world's ninth largest economy by honoring debt commitments and keeping low inflation policies, in addition to pushing for swift changes to Brazil's cumbersome tax and public pension systems, which have long been desired by investors. \par \par \uc2  After Sunday's win he also pledged to quickly address the gaping social divide in one of the world's most unequal societies. He is planning to create a special secretariat to address the plight of Brazil's 50 million people who live below the poverty line. \par \par \uc2  But the rapid naming of the economic team was key. The head of the Cardoso government's transition task force to the new administration also urged Lula to name his team quickly. \par \par \uc2  "We believe the sooner they name the economic team the better it will be for markets and investors," Pedro Parente told Globo television on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Lula won 61 percent of the vote in Sunday's runoff election against candidate Jose Serra of Cardoso's centrist ruling coalition, realizing a 22-year dream for his Workers' Party which was created during the country's 1964-85 military rule. \par \par \uc2  The 52.8 million votes won by Lula on Sunday made him the most popular elected president in Brazil's history, giving him a strong mandate to rule this vast country, stretching from the Amazon in the north to massive industrial cities in the south. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021029dyat0131s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
