\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Spent $1.2B On Spot Mkt To Boost Real In Sept \b0\par\par\uc2 370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2002\par 03:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent $1.2 billion on \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in September, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The September dollar sales brought total spot market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June to $4.1 billion. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimated last week that it would spend a total of about $9 billion with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The latest large \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the spot market came Monday when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected $350 million into the local market after the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bottomed out at 3.94 reals per dollar ahead of the expiration of about $1 billion  in dollar-linked debt. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's real has lost more than 35% against the dollar this year amid investor nervousness about the outcome of the  country's upcoming presidential elections scheduled for Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monetary Policy Director Luis Fernando Figueiredo, the worst of market anxiety should pass this week. "My vision is that we have passed a climax of anxiety and that after election day we'll  see a calming that will facilitate the rollover of dollar-linked debt," Figueiredo said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  however, said it has two more large debt expirations coming due before a possible second round runoff election Oct. 27. \par \par \uc2  About $3.6 billion in debt is scheduled to come due Oct. 17 and another $1.9 billion will come due Oct. 23. \par \par \uc2  Some critics of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy note that market players tend to bid up the dollar ahead of such expirations to guarantee higher returns. \par \par \uc2  Figueiredo said however the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has found no evidence that large institutional investors were manipulating the exchange rate unduly in their favor. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue it efforts to try to "dilute" the effect of events such as debt  expirations on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility. \par \par \uc2  "We are trying to maintain a volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that  is responsible," he said. "We can't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a level that will deplete our reserves for future use excessively. We have to guarantee the solvency of the country." \par \par \uc2  -By Gerald Jeffris, Dow Jones Newswires; 5561-9965-6883 gerald.effris@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021002dya20002c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
