\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Real Ends Stronger At BRL3.760/Dlr Vs BRL3.875 \b0\par\par\uc2 516 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2002\par 21:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Dashing expectations that the real would slip to BRL4.00 per dollar on worries that Workers' Party presidential candidate Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva is moving closer to an Oct. 6 victory, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily Monday to help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finish 3.1% stronger. \par \par \uc2  The real ended at BRL3.760 per dollar, compared with BRL3.875 at Friday's close. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it sold dollars on the spot market, but didn't disclose the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as is customary. \par \par \uc2  Traders, however, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  acted in larger volumes than usual. One \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  consultancy put the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  at $300 million, about triple the amount of other recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was active throughout the session, selling some $43.3 million in export credit lines and buying back BRL110 million in floating-rate bills to help stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets. \par \par \uc2  Traders had expected the real to fall on polls released over the weekend and early Monday that showed Lula's support base is rising, and on a view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  would fail to roll over a large batch of dollar-linked debt set to mature Tuesday. Paying off the debt would boost the amount of reals in circulation in a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that already has too few dollars. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be acting to squeeze any speculators out of the market and offer liquidity to firms buying dollars to service external debts. \par \par \uc2  "This was a very crazy day," said one Sao Paulo trader, noting that the real opened 2.1% weaker at BRL3.96 per dollar Monday, its weakest-ever intraday level since being introduced in 1994 to stamp out hyperinflation. \par \par \uc2  While analysts expect extreme volatility to continue for the rest of this week, key members of Brazil's business and political community are saying positive things about Lula to help calm investors' nerves about a victory that looks increasingly likely. \par \par \uc2  Monday, President Fernando Henrique Cardoso's wife Ruth said a Lula presidency "wouldn't be a disaster" and Roberto Setubal, the head of banking giant Banco Itau SA (ITU), called Lula a pragmatist, instead of a left-wing ideologue. \par \par \uc2  Speaking in Washington, Setubal said Lula "will be the next president for sure." He also said the recent selloff of the real amounts to an "overreaction" by the market and said Brazil's banking system is "well prepared" for a political transition. \par \par \uc2  The comments by the first lady and Setubal complemented a growing list of endorsements Lula has received in recent weeks from some members of Brazil's business community. \par \par \uc2  Monday Lula reiterated commitments to honoring all international contracts and primary budget surplus targets. \par \par \uc2  -By Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1479; terry.wade@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Despite Monday's rally, the real lost 23% of its value against the dollar in the third quarter of 2002. \par \par \uc2  -Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1479; 