\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Ends Weaker Despite Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 330 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2002\par 22:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real ended weaker Tuesday, despite a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the spot market to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. \par \par \uc2  The real ended at BRL3.137 per dollar, compared with BRL3.100 at Monday's close. The real lost as much as 1.8% Tuesday after rallying 1.9% in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  "Things are very volatile and there won't be clear trend in the real until the elections are over," said a Rio  de Janeiro-based trader. \par \par \uc2  He added that the dollar is still in relatively short supply on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, despite words of support from foreign banks that they will keep existing credit lines  open to Brazilian companies. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Pedro Malan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Arminio Fraga are meeting with European officials this week to ask them to boost trade financing to local exporters. \par \par \uc2  On the politics front, traders say they are pleased that government-backed candidate Jose Serra consolidated his second-place spot at about 20% in two polls Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But any gains in the real were held in check by frontrunner Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva, who hit 40% in the polls, leading some to speculate that he could win the presidency in a first-round vote Oct. 6 by gaining more than  50% of the vote on election day. The market hopes Serra will beat Lula in a second-round runoff Oct. 27. \par \par \uc2  The week dollar is keeping inflationary pressures relatively high, diminishing hopes that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take advantage of its negative bias on interest rates and cut the country's base rate of 18.0% in coming weeks. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Brazil's futures exchange, the BM&F said it will hold a minute of silence Wednesday at 1300 GMT to remember Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  -Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1479; terry.wade@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020910dy9a0019r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
