\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Real Ends At BRL3.152/Dlr Vs BRL3.120 Wednesday \b0\par\par\uc2 471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2002\par 21:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Growing fears of a U.S. invasion of Iraq ahead of the anniversary of the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks on the U.S. sent the Brazilian real weaker for the fourth session running Thursday, and investors are losing hope that news on the domestic front may reverse the declining trend in the near term. \par \par \uc2  The real closed at BRL3.152 to the dollar, near the weak point of the day of BRL3.160, despite a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the spot market and a sale of $52.5 million in six-month export credits. Wednesday, the real finished at BRL3.120. \par \par \uc2  Traders said most of Thursday's weakening followed worries about the global and domestic economic outlooks as there was little pressure on the spot market, unlike the three previous sessions. \par \par \uc2  "We saw signs of a bear market setting in today," said a Sao Paulo-based trader. \par \par \uc2  Going forward, traders expect volatility to continue in both directions, as war fears are likely to continue depressing global stock markets while the outlook for the Brazilian presidential elections coming up in October remains uncertain. \par \par \uc2  The real has shed 14.2 centavos since closing Friday at BRL3.010 to the dollar. So far this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 27% of its value. \par \par \uc2  As concern grows about the near future, traders are once  again questioning the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  firepower  to tame a possible bear market. \par \par \uc2  Under the $30 billion agreement with the International Monetary Fund, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed to sell dollars on the market - via spot sales or export credits - and buy back external debt up to $3 billion every moving 30-day period. \par \par \uc2  So far this month, market players estimate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent about $2.1 billion of the country's reserves in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buybacks. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international reserves, net of IMF funds, currently stand at  about $23.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  "What the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has left to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is enough to tame markets under relatively normal conditions, but not a market that's growing pessimistic," said another Sao Paulo-based trader. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, not even a relatively successful sale of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts managed to lift investors' spirits. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $716 million in 2002 and 2003 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, securing the rollover of 36% of a $1.97 billion lot of dollar-linked debt coming due Sept. 11. The rates on the swaps were high - between 24.50% and 27.49% - but in line with market rates. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has another three sessions to try  to refinance the rest of the maturing debt, increasing the likelihood that it may be successful. \par \par \uc2  -By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; (5511) 3145-1481; adriana.arai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020905dy950018t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
