\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Fraga: External Scenario Hampering Rate Cut \b0\par\par\uc2 589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2002\par 17:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Arminio Fraga Tuesday said an adverse global environment was  hampering a further reduction in the short-term benchmark rate of 18.00%. \par \par \uc2  Talking on a conference call sponsored by local press agency Agencia Estado, Fraga also said markets should be more aware that Brazil "will be able to manage its fate well." \par \par \uc2  "The global backdrop has gotten in our way but we are closely watching" how the external scenario develops, he said, referring to the possibility of a reduction in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The comments come as investors were starting to grow hopeful that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be able to deliver an intermeeting rate cut as soon as this week because of encouraging signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation and less volatility in the last week of August. \par \par \uc2  In August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the interest rates on hold but set a downward bias. \par \par \uc2  However, the real weakened Monday and Tuesday. Since Friday's close of BRL3.010 to the dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shed 2.9% of its value. It's a depreciation that Fraga attributed to a hostile global environment. \par \par \uc2  By 1355 GMT, the real was trading at BRL3.103 to the dollar after closing at BRL3.062. Meanwhile, the main Sao Paulo stocks index was trading 2.6% lower at 10,109 points. The markets were reacting to lower-than-expected U.S. manufacturing activity. \par \par \uc2  Fraga added the decision to set a downward bias for interest rates follows a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  2003 inflation forecast that was well below the government's 4% target for the year. \par \par \uc2  He wouldn't comment on the exact estimate. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said there are "preliminary signs" that foreign banks are resuming the supply of trade finance lines to Brazilian companies following his trip to New York last week. He wouldn't provide figures. \par \par \uc2  He added that it was an extremely positive sign to see low demand for trade credit lines being offered by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "This means that supply (of trade lines) is improving and also that there's a rhythm for the market to absorb (the lines provided by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to offer trade lines to local banks to arrest a severe overseas credit crunch at a time when foreign banks are holding back amid worry about Brazil's debt outlook and presidential elections in October. So far, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has placed nearly $500 million in trade credit lines. Officials said they could go beyond $2 billion in such offerings. \par \par \uc2  He also said Brazil could negotiate better terms with the International Monetary Fund for a $30 billion aid package and seek permission to dig deeper into the country's reserves to carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buy back debt. Under the current terms, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  committed to keeping its international reserves higher than $10 billion. \par \par \uc2  ""It would be a lot easier to renegotiate the floor for international reserves than seek more aid funds," he said, adding that there's a precedent within the IMF for a negative reserve floor "if the circumstances demand it." \par \par \uc2  He said such measures could be necessary in the event that global environment "remains dry and hostile." \par \par \uc2  Fraga also said the government's recent forecast that the primary budget surplus for 2002 would reach 3.9% of gross domestic product - compared with an IMF target of 3.75% - was just "fine tuning." \par \par \uc2  By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11 3145-1481 adriana.arai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020903dy930011w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
