\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging debt-Brazil inches upward as default fears subside. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nichola Groom \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2002\par 22:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Emerging market sovereign bonds inched upwards on Wednesday as profit-taking following Brazil's recently-announced $30 billion IMF package trailed off and investor fears of a near-term default on Brazilian debt appeared to subside. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's share of the J.P. Morgan Emerging Market Bond Index Plus rose 1.92 percent in terms of daily returns, while the broader market edged up 0.72 percent in thin trading. \par \par \uc2  Last week the International Monetary Fund delivered a $30 billion rescue package to Brazil, virtually guaranteeing the country's ability to meet its debt obligations at least through the remainder of 2002. \par \par \uc2  The IMF bailout gave temporary reprieve to Brazil's strapped bonds, which are down about 30 percent for the year on investor fears about the lead of leftist candidates in the October presidential elections, but the euphoria did not last long as nervous investors sparked a massive sell-off lasting well into this week. \par \par \uc2  "Prices as they are now, particularly in the debt market, are extremely low," said Ricardo Amorim, head of Latin American research at IDEAGlobal. "The risk of a default in Brazilian debt this year is virtually zero and I think that was why there was a small bounce." \par \par \uc2  Some analysts say Brazilian bond prices currently reflect a default probability of about 50 percent and were due for a rebound. \par \par \uc2  Knut Langholm, a senior vice-president at State Street Research, said he was not surprised to see an uptick in Brazil's bonds because the bout of profit-taking had largely run its course. \par \par \uc2  Many investors, however, preferred to sit on the sidelines, after Brazil's latest presidential polls showed market favorite Jose Serra's support stagnating at around 13 percent less than two months before the Oct. 6 election. \par \par \uc2  "It is just natural that since the polls are largely unchanged, volatility will decline," said Langholm. "The decline in volatility is consistent with slightly firmer prices." \par \par \uc2  A poll released Wednesday afternoon showed center-leftist Ciro Gomes closing the gap on front-runner Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva, while Serra remained wedged in a distant third with only 13.4 percent of the votes. A poll on Tuesday night showed similar results. \par \par \uc2  MARKET SHOULD STABILIZE \par \par \uc2  Brazil's benchmark C bonds were up 1.5 points at 53 and are expected to settle in the 50 to 55 range over the next few days, according to Langholm. \par \par \uc2  "I really don't see the polls changing that," he said. \par \par \uc2  Still, others are concerned the rally will be difficult to sustain. \par \par \uc2  "The markets still feel heavy and are trading without much conviction on the upside," Amorim said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  Serra's support in recent weeks has lagged that of the two leftist front-runners, Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and Ciro Gomes, by about 20 percentage points. \par \par \uc2  Investors are worried that Lula or Gomes, if elected, would undo critical economic policies and push the country toward a default on its $250 billion public debt-load. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the bonds were also propped up on talk \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to improve credit conditions for local companies. \par \par \uc2  "The use of net international reserves should be announced in the next few days and should help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilize, at least on a temporary basis," Morgan Stanley said in a Tuesday research report. \par \par \uc2  The market also welcomed news that all the leading candidates in the presidential race said on Tuesday they would meet with President Fernando Henrique Cardoso to discuss the country's economic state and lay the groundwork for a smooth transition of power in January. \par \par \uc2  "There have been a couple of local banks that are buying on speculation that the meetings will go well, and then you'll have some follow-on buying," said one trader. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020814dy8e010w8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
