\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Debt-Brazil slides as political woes weigh. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Susan Schneider \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2002\par 21:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Brazilian sovereign bonds slipped almost 2 percent in a see-saw session on Tuesday as persistent concerns about the sizable lead of two left-leaning candidates in October's presidential candidates curdled the mood of investors. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's share of the J.P. Morgan Emerging Market Bond Index Plus turned lower in late afternoon trading, falling 1.73 percent in terms of daily returns in the thinly traded session. The nation's benchmark C bond shed 0.625 points to 51.375 bid. \par \par \uc2  The slide came as investors continue to fret about the chasm separating Wall Street's favorite candidate, government-backed Jose Serra, and leftish candidates Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and Ciro Gomes. \par \par \uc2  "The relative dominance of political concerns over all else will probably continue to prevail, at least in the near term," said Mark Siegel, an emerging market fund manager with D.L. Babson & Co., a member of the MassMutual Financial Group. \par \par \uc2  Politics has been the overreaching theme in Brazilian markets for months and has returned to the spotlight in recent days, in spite of last week's $30 billion International Monetary Fund lifeline aimed at shoring up battered markets. \par \par \uc2  Investors are worried that Lula or Gomes, if elected, would mismanage the economy and push Brazil toward a default on its $250 billion debt load. Some analysts say Brazilian bond prices currently reflect a default probability of about 50 percent. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's bonds also swung on rumors that an election poll due out late on Tuesday would show Serra, now trailing his opponents in a distant third place by some 20 percentage points, making a bit of headway. \par \par \uc2  "There's a rumor that Serra will get 2 percent better in the next poll," said one emerging debt trader. "I don't think anyone's going to get too excited about that, but the volume is so thin it's tough to get a read for what's going on." \par \par \uc2  Traders in Sao Paulo said there was also speculation Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be buying up bonds, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  In the midst of the election-linked turmoil, ratings agency Moody's Investors Service late on Monday cut its ceiling for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds one notch to B2, or five notches below investment grade. \par \par \uc2  Moody's said the downgrade came amid concerns the incoming government will face growing fiscal pressures that could translate into risks in the external sector, particularly if a further weakening of Brazil's real increases the burden on dollar borrowers. \par \par \uc2  Moody's now has Brazil rated below Bolivia and Bulgaria and on par with Honduras, Venezuela and Lebanon. \par \par \uc2  The move by Moody's weighed on the real earlier on Tuesday, but bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors had largely looked past the news by late in the day. Traders said bonds were already trading at levels below the new Moody's rating. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't help, but I don't think it has a tremendous drive on taking prices lower right now," said another emerging debt trader. The volatile session came instead because "there are political concerns, there's poor liquidity in the market overall and there's uncertainty about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighed on the broader market, sinking the EMBI-Plus 0.50 percent in terms of daily returns and pressuring the neighbors of Latin America's largest economy. \par \par \uc2  Colombian debt lost 1.48 percent in terms of daily returns, Venezuela shed 0.38 percent and Peru lost 2.09 percent. \par \par \uc2  Even Mexico, seen as a relative safe haven as its close trade ties with the United States help to insulate it from Latin American fallout, slid 0.41 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020813dy8d011i4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
