\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil rejoices in record $30 bln IMF loan accord. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Axel Bugge \par \uc2 855 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2002\par 06:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRASILIA, Brazil, Aug 8 (Reuters) - Brazil's government and markets rejoiced on Thursday over a record $30 billion IMF loan that boosted global optimism that the country can smoothly ride out turbulent presidential elections. \par \par \uc2  Markets from New York to Europe rallied after the International Monetary Fund came to the rescue of Latin America's largest economy, which has struggled for months to restore eroding investor confidence ahead of October elections which the leftist opposition has strong chances of winning. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, plunged to record lows late last month, stocks fell and bond spreads spiraled as investors feared the hard-won economic stability of President Fernando Henrique Cardoso's government was under threat. \par \par \uc2  But with the IMF credit, which was much larger than expected, investors took the view that Brazil is now safe from turning into another Argentina, Brazil's southern neighbor which is in the grip of its worst economic crisis in memory. \par \par \uc2  "This is going to give markets what they need to recover and make a safe transition to a new government," said Roberto Dantas, an economist at Banco Schahin, a Sao Paulo brokerage. \par \par \uc2  The real closed 3.8 percent higher at 2.91 per dollar, after reaching 3.605 on July 31. Stocks ended 4.5 percent higher and Brazil's foreign bonds rose 5.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  "With this international aid, the next president will have a base, a support to consolidate his program and policies, and calm down investors, savers, citizens and society," \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Arminio Fraga told investors in a videoconference. "With this the economy will grow." \par \par \uc2  CARDOSO LEGACY AT STAKE? \par \par \uc2  Cardoso's low inflation policies, mass privatizations and controlled public spending helped make Brazil the second largest recipient of foreign direct investment in the emerging market universe in recent years. "This is not just a vote of confidence in the government but in the country, which has the ability to fulfill its commitments," Cardoso said. \par \par \uc2  Concerns that Cardoso's achievements could be undone by the incoming president have been a driving force behind investor worries and helped explain why many analysts were still reluctant to sound the all-clear on Brazil's economic health. \par \par \uc2  "There is uncertainty because these politicians don't have a track record and that is still a risk that continues," said Siobhan Manning, a Latin American debt strategist at the Italian investment bank Caboto. \par \par \uc2  Credit rating agency Standard & Poor's said on Thursday it retained a negative outlook on Brazil's ratings despite the IMF loan, saying doubts about the economy linger. \par \par \uc2  Still, the deal won supportive comments from rich nations. In a statement on behalf of the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations, Canada's Finance Minister John Manley said the G7 welcomed the agreement, adding it "should help restore market confidence." \par \par \uc2  Investors' central concern has been that the next president would be unable to handle Brazil's $250 billion public debt, the cost of which has soared as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slumped. \par \par \uc2  But IMF first deputy managing director Anne Krueger said Brazil's debt is sustainable and there were big differences between Brazil and Argentina. Argentina's debt default led some investors to believe Brazil could go the same way. \par \par \uc2  "There are a number of issues in Argentina which simply are not there in Brazil," Krueger said in Geneva. \par \par \uc2  A major reason why markets have worried about the debt burden - which represents just under 60 percent of economic output - is that leading left-wing presidential candidate Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva in the past had espoused renegotiating the terms of Brazil's public debt and frowned on IMF policies. \par \par \uc2  CANDIDATES SUPPORT IMF DEAL \par \par \uc2  But in a sign that Lula has changed his stance, his running mate, businessman Jose Alencar, told foreign correspondents on Thursday that: "The responsibility of this deal with the IMF is the responsibility of Brazil, not just this government." \par \par \uc2  Eighty percent of the $30 billion in new funds from the IMF would be disbursed in 2003, the first year of the new administration, as long as the next president meets a 2003 primary budget surplus target of 3.75 percent of economic output. Progress toward that target will be revised quarterly. \par \par \uc2  The primary budget surplus excludes debt-servicing costs. \par \par \uc2  Government candidate Jose Serra, who is lagging far behind in polls, and center-left former finance minister Ciro Gomes, have also indicated they support a deal with the IMF. \par \par \uc2  "Support of the main candidates for this deal will not be missing," Finance Minister Pedro Malan said. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said that with the IMF agreement, his country will halt daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the real but stood ready to step in whenever necessary. \par \par \uc2  Under the accord, the permitted floor on Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves was cut to $5 billion from $15 billion, giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  a much larger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  war chest. \par \par \uc2  Malan said \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also talking with the Inter-American Development Bank and the World Bank, but gave no details. \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Isabel Versiani, Claudia Pires, Katherine Baldwin). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020809dy89007kw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
