\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. Signals Its Support for Aid to Brazil \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael M. Phillips in Washington and Jonathan Karp in Sao Paulo, Brazil \par \uc2 855 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2002\par 05:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Staff Reporters of The Wall Street Journal \par \par \uc2  The Bush administration signaled it would support a financial rescue package for embattled Brazil, beating a retreat from its anti-bailout policy. \par \par \uc2  Shortly after he irked Brazilian President Fernando Henrique Cardoso by suggesting new aid might be siphoned off to Swiss bank accounts, and just days before his first official visit to the trouble spots of South America, Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill indicated the U.S. would support an agreement now being negotiated between Brasilia and the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  "The economic team in Brazil has done a remarkable job of maintaining sound fiscal and monetary policies," Mr. O'Neill said Thursday. "I continue to favor support for Brazil and other nations that take appropriate policy steps to build sound, sustainable and growing economies." \par \par \uc2  The announcement came as the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rocked for days by Mr. O'Neill's criticisms and investor worries about the country's political future and huge public debt, shot up almost 10% on news that talks between IMF and top Brazilian financial officials were going well. After a week that has witnessed panicked selling of Brazil's real, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed Thursday at 3.16 to the dollar, up from Wednesday's close of 3.47. Boosted by \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real posted its largest single-day percentage gain ever; Monday was the largest single-day loss. \par \par \uc2  "The main reason for the rise is the IMF," said Paulo Vieira da Cunha, senior vice president of Lehman Bros. in New York. "An agreement is likely to be achieved, but I don't think it will be imminent and it could yet disappoint the market." \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials stressed that their support for a Brazil rescue loan is contingent upon the Brazilian authorities' agreeing to continued sound spending and monetary policies. An IMF deal that extends into next year will also require reassurance from the main presidential candidates that they will honor the agreement reached by the current government. One of the factors distressing investors is the possibility that either of two left-wing candidates, Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and Ciro Gomes, will win in October's vote. \par \par \uc2  There was no word Thursday on how large the IMF loan package might be or how it would address the country's $264 billion public debt, which some economists say can be paid only if creditors accept a restructuring agreement. Mr. O'Neill avoided the question of whether a debt restructuring would be necessary to keep the country solvent. \par \par \uc2  Mr. O'Neill's willingness to back an IMF deal will likely make his trip to Brazil, Uruguay and Argentina, starting Sunday, more pleasant. The president is dispatching Mr. O'Neill to the region to demonstrate that his administration empathizes with its suffering, a claim that many Latin Americans had begun to doubt. Mr. O'Neill himself contributed to those doubts, hinting that corruption in the three nations was at the heart of their economic woes and casually suggesting to a reporter on a long elevator ride some weeks back that he opposed additional aid for Brazil. \par \par \uc2  The latest comment, about Swiss accounts, prompted the Jornal do Brasil newspaper to opine that Mr. O'Neill is as "polite and careful as a monkey in a china shop." \par \par \uc2  "What can we do?" said Rubens Barbosa, Brazil's ambassador in Washington. "The problem is, if the secretary of the treasury of Uganda said the same thing, nobody would pay attention. If the secretary of the Treasury here says it, it has an immediate effect." \par \par \uc2  Amid the slights, the administration has been trying to reassure Latin Americans that they are indeed the foreign-policy priority Mr. Bush claimed they were during his presidential campaign. Mr. O'Neill pointed to Thursday's Senate passage of a major trade bill as evidence that the U.S. is engaged with the region; the bill extends trade benefits to Andean nations and facilitates negotiations for a free-trade deal with Chile, as well as a hemisphere-wide trade zone. \par \par \uc2  And the Treasury conspicuously announced its enthusiastic support this week for a new or accelerated IMF loan for tiny, beleaguered Uruguay, whose banks have been sapped largely by Argentines desperate to get their money home. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. has been tougher on Argentina, which is in the throes of a complete economic meltdown and mired in difficult talks with the IMF to roll over some $10 billion in debt. Given past failures of Argentine economic plans, both IMF and U.S. officials take the government's promises of fiscal prudence with a grain or two of salt. \par \par \uc2  "The people of Argentina have suffered from the economic turmoil there," Mr. O'Neill said, "and we are eager to see that nation return to a position of strength and stability." \par \par \uc2  Write to Jonathan Karp at jonathan.karp@wsj.com and Michael M. Phillips at michael.phillips@wsj.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020802dy82001e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
