\par\par \b \uc2  Argentine peso posts strong gains, shares dip. \b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2002\par 00:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, Aug 1 (Reuters) - Argentina's peso firmed over 2 percent on Thursday due mostly to \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  while shares fell amid investor fears over debt-laden \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  and crisis-hit Uruguay. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's peso ended at 3.63/3.65 per dollar in large-scale transactions on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market , stronger than Wednesday's close of 3.71/3.73. \par \par \uc2  The peso is still down about 70 percent this year, though it gained 4.5 percent in July amid signs of economic stability after Argentina's chaotic four-year recession. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the peso's gains were due largely to very heavy selling of $70.6 million into the market by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Argentina's leading MerVal share index closed down 0.42 percent at 357.79 on brisk volume of 42.7 million pesos. The index is now up about 10 percent since the beginning of the year, having clawed back from sharp losses in recent months. \par \par \uc2  "Regional uncertainty is entrenched and all the bourses are paying the consequences," said one local trader. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's economy has been hammered by a run on banks, a dive in reserves and consumer spending, and the ripple effects of Argentina's own prolonged economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  Brazil, meanwhile, is being buffeted by fears that the regional giant could be pushed toward default on its $250 billion public debt if a leftist wins the October presidential election. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund said on Thursday that talks about a potential new loan for Argentina were going well, but it said more work remained before a new loan could be agreed. \par \par \uc2  Argentina has been in talks for months to restart aid from the IMF, and in recent weeks the pace of negotiations has quickened. The IMF cut off access to a $22 billion credit line in December after the government failed to control public spending. \par \par \uc2  The IMF also said it was in talks with Brazil to extend its current aid package through the end of 2003 but declined to comment on whether additional cash would be sent to the Latin American giant. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said it was approaching talks with Uruguay on how to deal with a banking crisis there with a sense of urgency but hinted that no deal was imminent. \par \par \uc2  Shares in Argentine energy group Perez Companc , the target of a $1.13 billion takeover bid by Brazil's Petrobras , gained slightly after its main unit announced a swap of nearly $1 billion in bonds had closed. \par \par \uc2  Perez shares are down roughly 18 percent since the deal, dependent on Perez restructuring its debt, was announced on July 22. Investors are worried about the buyout's effect on minority shareholders. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020801dy81015oh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
