\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  cenbank seen beefing up  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Todd Benson \par \uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2002\par 18:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to beef up in coming days its policy of selling at least $50 million daily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  As Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slumped to consecutive record lows in recent days, players are closely watching what course the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take when its current minimum level of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ends its month-long run on Wednesday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must at least double its sales to at least $100 million daily to arrest the downward spiral of the real. \par \par \uc2  In July, the bank fed the market with an estimated $1.5 billion, selling at least $50 million a day to boost liquidity and help prop up the flagging real , battered in part by investor nervousness over Brazil's October presidential vote. \par \par \uc2  Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily dollar injections, the real has shed about 9 percent of its value so far in July, plunging to six straight record low closes over the past week. Earlier Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a new all-time intraday low of 3.305 reais per dollar. \par \par \uc2  While the market welcomed what it dubbed the "daily ration" of dollars, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to at least double the amount to quench the market's thirst for greenbacks and ease pressure on the real. \par \par \uc2  "The market consensus is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to increase the amount to at least $100 million a day," said Bertrand Wanclik, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Itau Corretora, a brokerage in Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  Though the market expects the bank to announce a new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the coming days, no date has been set. \par \par \uc2  The bank's strategy may also depend on the outcome of talks between Brazilian officials and the International Monetary Fund starting Wednesday in Washington. \par \par \uc2  "Even more important than increasing the so-called daily ration of dollars is some sort of deal with the IMF," said Marcelo Carvalho, chief economist at Bank of America in Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  "Without that, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not have enough ammunition to satisfy the market" in the near term, he added. \par \par \uc2  Under Brazil's current IMF agreement, which expires by December, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is required to maintain a minimum of $15 billion in reserves, thereby limiting the amount of dollars it can inject into the market. \par \par \uc2  In a bid to soothe market jitters and shore up more cash, the government announced late Monday that it is sending a high-level team to Washington to further talks already underway with the Fund. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Arminio Fraga said last week Brazil would seek a new IMF loan deal if market turbulence persists. \par \par \uc2  With the approach of Brazil's presidential elections, dollars have been in short supply in recent weeks as the market frets over opinion polls, which show the government candidate and market favorite trailing in a distant third behind two left-of-center candidates. \par \par \uc2  Investors fear that a left-wing president would be more likely to resort to populist economic measures in the event of a debt crunch next year. Brazil's public debt is $250 billion, about 58 percent of the country's gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020730dy7u00tyq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
