\par\par \b \uc2  Roaring Equities Boost Dollar, While Brazilian Real Plunges \b0\par\par\uc2 1,120 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2002\par 00:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar posted solid gains against both the euro and the yen Monday as U.S. equities surged on a storm of bargain hunting, but the greenback had to share the limelight with the Brazilian real, which sank amid a stampede of selling by domestic accounts. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday in New York, the dollar was at 119.74 yen, close to its intraday high and tantalizingly close to the key 120 yen mark, up from 118.94 yen late Friday. The dollar soared as high as 119.97 yen, but lacked momentum to make a decisive breakthrough. \par \par \uc2  Should it breach and remain above 120 yen, then stop-loss orders would likely be triggered giving the dollar momentum to quickly reach as high as 120.50 yen or even 121 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 98.00 U.S. cents, down from 98.74 cents Friday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at 1.4876 Swiss franc, up from 1.4650 francs. Sterling was at $1.5617, down from $1.5645. \par \par \uc2  While the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended last week's rally, the real teetered on the verge of collapse. \par \par \uc2  "It's panic," said Gustavo Rangel, an emerging markets strategist with Barclays Capital in New York. He added Monday's fall of the Brazilian real to record lows, breaching 3.2 Brazilian reals to the dollar, was "mostly [driven by] demand for physical dollars by corporates in Brazil." \par \par \uc2  Previously, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants had anticipated there would soon be some $10 billion of International Monetary Fund money flowing to Brazil, Latin America's biggest economy, but U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill over the weekend "said basically it would not be forthcoming in the near term," commented Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The pressure on Brazil is real. To shrug it off as just a speculative attack obscures the underlying problems," the country faces, he added. "Brazilians themselves are taking money out of the country." \par \par \uc2  The frantic \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market action in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , didn't cause widespread turmoil in emerging markets Monday, but it did help the dollar recapture something of its allure as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of refuge. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rejoined the trajectory of equity markets and hitched a ride on soaring U.S. stocks to trade at three-week highs against the euro and the yen. This caused some wavering among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants who have been betting the greenback is trapped in a decline. \par \par \uc2  "The jury is still out," on whether the dollar's short-term recovery may turn into something more enduring, said Grant Wilson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh. \par \par \uc2  In the past week, the dollar has sprung back more than 3% against both the Japanese and the common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That pares the dollar's losses for 2002 to about 9% against the yen and around 10% versus the euro. \par \par \uc2  Analysts called attention to the stronger performance of some of the so-called "commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " Monday. Earlier this month, the stumble of the Australian, Canadian and New Zealand dollars had heralded a brightening of dollar sentiment. Now, these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' recovery may indicate the U.S. dollar's short-lived rally may be about to play itself out, some said. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, the Australian dollar was trading at 54.05 U.S. cents, up from 53.59 cents late in New York Friday. Against the Canadian dollar, the U.S. dollar was at $1.5721 Canadian, down from C$1.5868 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar ended sharply higher, boosted by news that Standard & Poor's upgraded Canada's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign credit rating to AAA from AA plus, market watchers said. Stabilizing equity markets and reduced risk aversion also helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover from last week's plummet. \par \par \uc2  After New Zealand's elections over the weekend, in which the Labour party had increased its share of the vote and appeared in a strong enough position to form another minority coalition government, the New Zealand dollar was trading at 46.85 U.S. cents late Monday in New York, up from 46.25 cents in late New York trading Friday. \par \par \uc2  "If we see stabilization in those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that would be evidence that the U.S. dollar recovery ... is approaching its end," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with IDEAGlobal in New York. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, some technical factors indicated that the until-recently gloomy U.S. dollar sentiment might have turned on its heel and have embarked on a more upbeat march for a while to come. \par \par \uc2  "From a technical perspective, the dollar is finding more support," while after four straight sessions of U.S. stock averages gaining, "the dollar market is settling," said James McCormick, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Lehman Brothers in New York. \par \par \uc2  He noted his firm's "Bull and Bear Model" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market sentiment index showed the dollar's bearish trend that started in April -- the longest-running since 1995 -- flipped into bullish mode on Friday for the first time in four months. \par \par \uc2  A key factor driving the dollar higher appears to be the crumbling of the yen, which started last week and helped ignite the greenback's ascent against other widely-traded rivals. \par \par \uc2  Newfound concerns about Japan's financial sector could enable the dollar to rise to between 120 yen and 122 yen in the very short term, wrote Robert Sinche, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Citibank in New York in a research note Monday. Japan's banks are burdened with 150 trillion yen of bad and potentially non-performing loans and are also heavily exposed to the domestic stock market. \par \par \uc2  Longer-term dollar-negative factors including the burgeoning U.S. current account deficit and a cloud of doubt remaining about U.S. corporate accounting which may yet return to plague the dollar, some warned. \par \par \uc2  As the Aug. 14 deadline by which U.S. chief executives must certify their financial statements approaches, a sense of unease about whether more flaws in corporate accounts will surface in the coming weeks "is tilted in the direction of bad news," and it has the potential to defuse the dollar's current rebound, said HSBC's Mr. Chandler. \par \par \uc2  "The stock market has been moving up, but let's put this in perspective," he noted, adding U.S. stock averages are still much lower than a month ago. "The downside risks" to U.S. asset prices "are for real." \par \par \uc2  (Adriana Arai in Sao Paulo contributed to this report) \par \par \uc2  Write to John Parry at john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc