\par\par \b \uc2  O'Neill Comments, Local Selling Thrash Brazilian Real \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton and John Parry \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2002\par 21:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The Brazilian real plunged to its lowest levels ever against the dollar Monday, as worries over a U.S. official's comments over the weekend were compounded by widespread selling of South America's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Still, market professionals downplayed talk that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators were jumping on board, furthering the pressure on the real. \par \par \uc2  "I hardly think this is speculative; that's not the key problem," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with HSBC in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The pressure on Brazil is real: To shrug it off as just a speculative attack obscures the underlying problems," said Chandler. "Brazilians themselves are taking money out of the country," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened to as far as BRL3.29 per dollar in afternoon trade, before rallying to BRL3.185 in late activity after the central market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market, according to traders in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But that was still far weaker than Friday's close of BRL3.105. \par \par \uc2  The real's latest problems began with "(Treasury Secretary Paul) O'Neill's comments about not going to Argentina and Brazil with aid packages," the head of one New York \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk said. \par \par \uc2  O'Neill, who will visit Argentina and Brazil in early August, said he won't offer either country a bailout at that time. "These are important friends and allies of the United States," he said on 'Fox News Sunday'. "Critically, they need to put in place policies that would ensure that as assistance money comes it does some good and it doesn't just go out of the country to Swiss bank accounts." \par \par \uc2  Previously, market participants had anticipated that there would be $10 billion of International Monetary Fund money coming to Brazil, but O'Neill over the weekend "basically said it would not be forthcoming in the near term," according to HSBC's Chandler. \par \par \uc2  Brazilian Finance Minister Pedro Malan early Monday sought to soothe markets by announcing that a fresh IMF program is on its way. But his remarks fell on deaf ears, as electoral and financing concerns pushed Brazil's bond spreads into uncharted territory and sent the real plunging to new historic lows. \par \par \uc2  `It's panic," said Gustavo Rangel, a strategist with Barclays Capital in New York. The fall of the Brazilian real Monday was "mostly (driven by) demand for physical dollars by corporates in Brazil that need to pay their amortizations," he said. \par \par \uc2  The slide of the real to another historic low, beyond BRL3.20 to the dollar Monday, surprised strategists. Rangel has a forecast for the BRL3.40 by late September, but now wonders if that target will be reached sooner. \par \par \uc2  He was also skeptical about the chances of IMF funding anytime soon. Such funding would only be likely if all candidates in the October presidential elections agreed beforehand to sign an agreement on economic policy with the IMF, a scenario Rangel said was difficult to imagine. \par \par \uc2  Government-backed candidate Jose Serra, a market favorite, is trailing two left-leaning presidential hopefuls in opinion polls ahead of the Oct. 6 election. President Fernando Henrique Cardoso is ineligible to run for a third straight term. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's debt-servicing costs have skyrocketed in recent months. About 80% of Brazil's domestic debt is tied to the dollar or the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark Selic lending rate, which currently stands at 18%. \par \par \uc2  The real has lost more than 20% against the dollar so far this year. \par \par \uc2  -By Tyler Lifton and John Parry, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248, 201-938-2096; tyler.lifton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Joe Rebello in Washington D.C. and Angela Pruitt in New York contributed to this story) \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020729dy7t00ayh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
