\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Real Ends At BRL2.995/Dollar; Worst Close Ever \b0\par\par\uc2 500 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2002\par 21:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Brazil's real plunged Thursday, breaching for the first time ever the psychologically-important barrier of BRL3.000 to the dollar during intraday trade amid mounting concerns about the country's ability to stay afloat and unstable U.S. equity markets. \par \par \uc2  The real hit an intraday low of BRL3.030 to the dollar before settling at BRL2.995 to the dollar - its weakest closing ever for the fourth session in a row. This is the eighth time in a month that the real has set a new record closing low. On Wednesday, the real finished at BRL2.946. \par \par \uc2  Market players said investors were increasingly nervous about the political platforms of presidential candidates ahead of elections in October. \par \par \uc2  "Not even an accord with the International Monetary Fund would help right now. That would only bring a temporary  relief to the market," said Solange Frour, senior economist at Mellon Global Investment in Rio de Janeiro. \par \par \uc2  Frour said there are candidates defending contradictory positions, such as keeping the fiscal budget in check while also raising the minimum wage. \par \par \uc2  "The social security system's deficit continues to grow, so it's absurd to even think of raising spending now," she added. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Brazil's net public debt hit a record high for the second month running, reaching 58.6% of gross domestic product at the end of June.  A bank official said the ratio is expected to increase a bit further in July due to "transitory factors," adding that the debt is sustainable. \par \par \uc2  These domestic worries are being compounded by weak U.S.  stock markets following a slew of corporate accounting scandals. \par \par \uc2  The real was also being pressured lower by rumors that an Ibope presidential poll slated to be unveiled Thursday evening could show Ciro Gomes, of the center-left Labor Front coalition, growing in popularity ahead of government-backed candidate Jose Serra, considered market-friendly. \par \par \uc2  Gomes has previously talked about a "voluntary lengthening" of Brazil's public debt, which has left some investors suspicious of him, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Market players were speculating the poll could show Gomes rising to 26-28%  from 20%, while Serra is seen dropping to 13% from 15% in a previous poll. \par \par \uc2  Opposition-candidate Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva was expected to show a slight drop in popularity, to 32% from 33%, while voting intentions for former  Rio de Janeiro governor Anthony Garotinho probably remained stable at 10% to 11%. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday sold $220 million, three-month dollar repurchase agreements at a rate of BRL3.05 a dollar. The deal was just a renewal of expiring repos. \par \par \uc2  Separately, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market with $50 million in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This is part of a daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program designed to contain the real's decline. \par \par \uc2  -By Paula Lace, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1480; brazil@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020725dy7p001ah\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
