\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Fraga Describes IMF Meetings As 'Good' \b0\par\par\uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2002\par 22:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Arminio Fraga, his country's financial markets in turmoil ahead of an October presidential election, said Thursday that he received strong signals of support during recent high-level meetings in Washington. \par \par \uc2  "I see Brazil being supported...by the IMF, by our fellow partners in the global economy in G7," Fraga told a group of investors in Manhattan after meeting Wednesday with officials from the International Monetary Fund and U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill. \par \par \uc2  Brazil recently drew down $10 billion from a $15.5 billion IMF credit line that expires in December. Fraga, who described the Washington meetings as "good," said more help could be on the way, if necessary. \par \par \uc2  "I do see many possibilities going forward," he said. Fraga was speaking during a presentation to investors hosted by Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  Fraga downplayed recent market speculation that a transitional IMF accord is in the works, which would involve Brazil's four leading presidential candidates agreeing to common macroeconomic targets. \par \par \uc2  "My expectation is that that is not the way we're going," he said. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bonds and stocks have been punished in past weeks amid opinion polls showing leftist candidate Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva with a commanding lead. \par \par \uc2  Fraga, however, insisted there are "no signs of big outflows" with many Brazilians shifting their funds to local certificates of deposit and into savings accounts. The biggest single monthly outflow had been around $100 million, he added. \par \par \uc2  "In a country our size, that is nothing," Fraga said. \par \par \uc2  He denied media reports that Brazil is seeking commitments from international banks on commercial credit lines, saying the government had made "no request of any kind." \par \par \uc2  "This isn't 1999," Fraga said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. banks, in a show of public support, agreed to maintain commercial credit lines in early 1999 after Brazil was forced to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , plunging Latin American markets into turmoil. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to break the fall of the real ($1=BRL2.794), a strategy that had begun to pay dividends. It has also maintained its benchmark interest rates at 18.50%, despite single-digit inflation. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said authorities are dedicated to tightening fiscal and monetary policy as much as is needed to calm jittery investors. \par \par \uc2  "We can go as far as I see necessary for this uncertainty to go away," Fraga said. \par \par \uc2  Fraga added that he believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued "if you take a long-term view." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has been buying back its bonds on the open market to soothe concerns about its heavy debt load. Fraga said the bank has repurchased about two-thirds of the planned $3 billion bond buyback. \par \par \uc2  The central banker said that whoever wins the presidential elections in October will inherit healthy economic fundamentals, including low inflation, solid banks and rising productivity. \par \par \uc2  "Whoever comes next will benefit from a lot of the work that has been done," Fraga said. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said he had briefed Aloysio Mercadante, the economic adviser to Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva, on his meetings in Washington. \par \par \uc2  Fraga promised to work with all the presidential candidates to ensure a smooth transition. \par \par \uc2  -By Mike Esterl, Dow Jones Newsiwires; 201-938-4026; Mike.Esterl@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020711dy7b008n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
