\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED -  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  real firm on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  plan, stocks drop. \b0\par\par\uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2002\par 23:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      In SAO PAULO story headlined "\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real firm on \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan, stocks drop," please read in fifth paragraph \par \par \uc2   ... to cover in July after covering about $2.5 billion in June \par \par \uc2   ... instead of ... to cover in June after covering about $2.5 \par \par \uc2   billion in July ..." (Correcting respective months) \par \par \uc2  A corrected version follows: \par \par \uc2  By Nicholas Winning \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Brazil, July 3 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened from record low territory on Wednesday as players bet a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan could break its seemingly interminable slide on local election and debt fears, traders said. \par \par \uc2  But hard-hit stock prices weakened further after Tuesday's Brazil sovereign rating cut by Standard & Poor's (S&P) and Wall Street volatility reinforced fears of reduced overseas investment in emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's real reversed early losses to close at 2.862 per dollar from Tuesday's close of 2.895, leaving it 19.1 percent below where it began 2002 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a total of $1.5 billion in July. \par \par \uc2  "In the short term, it should give it (market) a good boost and the market should calm down," ING Barings director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Diniz Pignatari said of the plan. "I think it (real) could stabilize around 2.80 in the short term." \par \par \uc2  New election polls could unsettle markets, but pressure on the real from companies should ease as they only had some $1.1 billion in overseas debt commitments to cover in July after covering about $2.5 billion in June, he said. \par \par \uc2  Just one day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at par with Monday's record low, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would pump $100 million into the market every day this week and then daily injections of $50 million for the rest of the month either on the spot market or with repurchase agreements. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday it sold all $100 million offered in an auction priced at 2.855 reais per dollar with a commitment to buy it back on Oct. 2 at 2.955 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The resumption of daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which were used in the second half of 2001 to stave off the impact of neighboring Argentina's economic crisis, aim to dull the impact of concerns about October's elections and meet local corporate needs. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the benchmark Bovespa stock index fell 1.9 percent to 10,635.5 points, or 21.7 percent weaker than where it began 2002. Losers outweighed gainers by 51 to six. \par \par \uc2  Telephone company Telemar , the market's heaviest-weighted stock with about 15 percent of the index, fell 1.2 percent drop to 26.41 reais. State oil giant Petrobras , the second-heaviest weighted stock, fell 1 percent to 46.81 reais. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's markets dove in June on Wall Street weakness and fears of mismanagement of the Brazil's economy and its $250 billion debt load should leftist presidential candidate Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva turn his big poll lead into a victory. \par \par \uc2  The Sao Paulo Stock Exchange said earlier that it had a $351 million net foreign investment outflow in June, its largest monthly record since December 1998. \par \par \uc2  Unlike the real, Brazilian stocks received no respite on Wednesday suffered in the wake of the S&P downgrade and ongoing Wall Street volatility and fears of new accounting scandals. \par \par \uc2  S&P cut Brazil's sovereign rating citing growing difficulties Latin America's biggest economy could manage its debt as fears of a Lula presidential election victory raised debt interest rates and servicing costs. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Thursday's Independence holiday in the United States would leave the Bovespa rudderless. \par \par \uc2  "Tomorrow will complicated as there won't be Bradys (bonds) so volume will be light and there won't be a reference point," said Concordia brokearge trader Tomi Taterka. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020703dy73014a7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
