\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  real firm on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  plan, stocks soft. \b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2002\par 16:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced on Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan one day after Standard & Poor's (S&P) lowered the country's rating due to election and debt fears. \par \par \uc2  Hard-hit stock prices weakened, however, as Wall Street weakness and concerns of more U.S. accounting scandals dictated the mood on the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange (Bovespa) for fear of reduced overseas investment in emerging markets like Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's real BRBY) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversed early losses after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with $1.5 billion in July, pumping a $100 million into the market every day for the rest of this week and then daily injections of $50 million until the end of the month. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar lost itself with the news of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and retreated rapidly, but the spread is very wide and the market has no reference," one local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said. \par \par \uc2  The real firmed to 2.845 per dollar from Tuesday's close of 2.895, the record low finish close chalked up on Monday, leaving it 18.6 percent below where it began the year. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , introduced in 1994, chalked up new intraday record low of 2.940 per dollar on Tuesday on a mix of concerns about a possible left-wing victory in October's presidential elections, corporate demand for dollars to pay overseas debts, speculation, and Wall Street weakness. \par \par \uc2  The Bovespa index .BVSP) dipped 0.5 percent to 10,790.3 points, or 20.5 percent weaker than where it began the year. Turnover was low with players expecting another thin session. \par \par \uc2  Phone company Telemar, the market's heaviest-weighted stock with about 15 percent of the index, firmed 0.3 percent drop to 26.80 reais. But state oil giant Petrobras, the markets second-heaviest weighted stock, fell 0.6 percent to 47.00 reais. \par \par \uc2  "I think yesterday it was clear that the market was much more linked to the U.S. than to the political situation here," said Bradesco Brokerage trader Francisco Andrade. "What will determine the Brazilian market is the U.S. market." \par \par \uc2  Brazil's markets are also suffering from underlying concerns of mismanagement of the Brazil's economy and its $250 billion debt load should leftist presidential candidate Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva win the October elections. \par \par \uc2  S&P cut Brazil's sovereign rating cited growing difficulties Brazil could manage its debt as the Lula worries raised servicing costs by lifting interest rate to levels not seen in more than three years. \par \par \uc2  The S&P move makes Brazilian debt less attractive to fund managers, some of which are restricted in holding debt rating agencies deem as being more risky, but local players said they expected little fallout from the move. \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Rodrigo Menderico). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020703dy7300spi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
