\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Rally Fizzles As Market Seeks Actions, Not Words \b0\par\par\uc2  By Terry Wade and Adriana Arai \par \uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2002\par 21:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Brazil's financial markets gave back some gains that came as the government Thursday unveiled a host of policies to shore up the country's fiscal position, with investors daring the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market instead of just talking about it. \par \par \uc2  In late trading, Brazil's real was at BRR2.71 a dollar, weaker than its strongest point Thursday of BRR2.640, but better than BRR2.795 at Wednesday's close, a nine-month low. The country's main stocks index was down 0.5% at 12,064 points after being up about 1% shortly after the government announced its measures. \par \par \uc2  Strong follow-through from the government may help. But the current administration can do little to assuage investor concerns that the next president - set to take office in January - will continue to pursue this government's economic stability goals. Finance Minister Pedro Malan said Thursday voters will demand sound fiscal policies when they cast their ballots in October, but investors aren't so sure. \par \par \uc2  "The country's now much better equipped to fight fires and the next possible rounds of panic selling will likely be less intense and shorter," said Alexandre Mathias, chief economist at Unibanco Asset Management. "But that doesn't mean they've resolved the election worries." \par \par \uc2  Currently, left-wing Workers' Party candidate Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva has a commanding 20 percentage point lead over government-backed candidate Jose Serra. Though he vows to maintain stability, the former union leader's past anti-market talk and signs of internal party divisions on key economic policy issues unnerve some investors. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon catch the shorts leaning the wrong way as the government is pulling out stops on a variety of fronts to allay investor fears about the outcome of elections and the country's ability to manage its hefty debt burden. \par \par \uc2  Merrill Lynch's Yianos Kontopoulous put out a research note Thursday afternoon recommending that investors go long real, saying: "The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  arsenal (size and flexibility) is an order of difference stronger than it was in last year's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cycle (which was successful)." \par \par \uc2  He added: "Some in the market are saying they heard the general outline of the policy response but not the specifics, thus, maybe the authorities are bluffing. Well, they are most likely not." \par \par \uc2  The arsenal includes about $10 billion from existing IMF credit lines by next week, a raising of the government's primary surplus target to 3.75% of GDP from 3.5% in 2002, a $3 billion buyback program for global bonds and the freeing up of an added $5 billion in reserves for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the spot market. \par \par \uc2  But in this jittery environment, traders are hoping to see these \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  occur as soon as possible and aren't happy about waiting around a few days for formal sign-offs from the IMF. \par \par \uc2  About half of the $10 billion Brazil hopes to draw from the IMF needs formal approval next week, along with a lowering of the country's international reserves floor to $15 billion from $20 billion to free up cash to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Once approved, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have around $10 billion in cash for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up from less than $4 billion. \par \par \uc2  Traders are also anxious to see the government start buying back about $3 billion of the $9 billion in dollar-denominated global bonds maturing in 2003 and 2004. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Arminio Fraga said the buybacks will provide a double dose of relief by cutting debt servicing costs and making it easier and cheaper for Brazilian companies to continue borrowing overseas. \par \par \uc2  -By Terry Wade and Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1479; brazil@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020613dy6d0017y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
