\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Malan: Ups Primary Surplus Target To 3.75% In '02 \b0\par\par\uc2 1,133 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2002\par 17:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- Two months after paying back nearly a third of its $15 billion International Monetary Fund loan brokered last year, Finance Minister Pedro Malan confirmed Brazil would draw down $10 billion from the existing program. \par \par \uc2  As part of an effort to calm financial markets amid worries about Brazil's future debt obligations, Malan said the Brazilian government is further tightening its grip on fiscal accounts in this election year. \par \par \uc2  Speaking at a press conference in Brasilia, the finance minister said the government is raising this year's consolidated public primary budget surplus target to 3.75% of gross domestic product from 3.50%. \par \par \uc2  Also, Malan said Brazil would seek permission from the IMF for additional room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  is looking to lower the floor on its net international reserves to $15 billion from $20 billion. \par \par \uc2  Looking to alleviate market concern about Brazil's debt outlook, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Arminio Fraga at the same press conference said Brazil would engage in an international debt buyback program. \par \par \uc2  The program will involve spending up to $3 billion to retire 2003 and 2004 debt. \par \par \uc2  Fraga also reiterated recent government comments that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when necessary, by making use of the usual tools at hand. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually sells dollars on the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at times of high volatility or low liquidity. Seeking to reassure investors, Fraga added that June foreign capital outflows have so far been smaller than in May. \par \par \uc2  Ahead of the confirmation, market players said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be smart to draw down the country's pre-approved credit lines and boost its reserves as investors worry about Brazil's ability to meet future debt obligations. \par \par \uc2  Fraga spent this week in Washington, chairing an IMF seminar, while Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened to levels not seen since the Sept. 11 attacks on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is looking to boost its gross reserves which currently stand at about $33 billion. Net of IMF funds, reserves stand at a total $28 billion. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to swap medium- and long-term debt for short-term securities to help further calm the markets. The same applies for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts. \par \par \uc2  The exchanges would continue to involve floating-rate LFT treasury bills and dollar-linked NTN-D notes. \par \par \uc2  Finance Deputy Minister Amaury Bier said the government wouldn't need to take additional fiscal measures to increase its primary budget surplus this year. \par \par \uc2  He said a May government decision to withhold as much as BRR5.3 billion in public spending plus continued fiscal  discipline on regional governments would ensure Brazil meets its new 3.75% goal. \par \par \uc2  "If additional (federal) effort were necessary, we will do it," Bier said. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said the current situation is manageable, "without having to pull the rabbit out of the hat." Fine-tuning would continue on the open market, he added. \par \par \uc2  Fraga also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would resume offering 60-day external lines of credit to banks, as was done during the transition from 2000 to 2001. This transaction, he said, means that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  will sell dollars and buy reals from banks for a period of 60 days. \par \par \uc2  He added that a senior-ranking IMF team will recommend that the Fund's board next week approve the country's request to dip deeper into international reserves to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if necessary. \par \par \uc2  The IMF's board is expected to approve Brazil's latest performance review next week, which would allow the country to draw down on $5 billion in aid funds. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said the current turbulence in financial markets, which Wednesday sent the real close to record lows, is rooted in "anxiety in relation to the future" as the presidential election approaches. \par \par \uc2  Such jitters would go away once markets are confident that "the country's commitment to fiscal responsibility won't go straight out of the window," he said, in a veiled reference to the opposition Workers' Party's current lack of commitment to fiscal austerity. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said he expected the IMF to approve the change to the floor on net international reserves next week. \par \par \uc2  Talking about debt maturing in 2003 and 2004, Fraga said $3.5 billion would come due in 2003, and $5.5 billion in 2004. \par \par \uc2  Clarifying the government position on the primary budget surplus target change, Planning Minister Guilherme Dias said the decision to further tighten fiscal policy was the government's own move, and the higher target wouldn't be part of the IMF program. He added that the 3.75% primary budget surplus corresponds to about BRR48.7 billion ($1=BRR2.795). \par \par \uc2  Bier emphasized that the IMF-set 3.5% primary budget surplus target would remain in place until the end of September, when the current accord closes. But Brazil will then go into fiscal austerity overdrive for the remainder of the year to try and meet the full 2002 target of 3.75%. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said he would risk saying that "the real is now undervalued and should strengthen in nominal and real terms in the long run." \par \par \uc2  He said the external debt buyback program, in addition to being "a noble use for the country's reserves," will also help lower short-term spreads and pave the way for Brazilian companies to continue borrowing overseas. \par \par \uc2  Confirming expectations that the government wouldn't look to broker a 2003 accord with the IMF, Malan said it would be up to the next administration to decide on such a matter. \par \par \uc2  Brazil has a reputation for meeting IMF targets and has consistently fulfilled its goals since the end of 1998. \par \par \uc2  In April, Brazil smashed market expectations and posted a consolidated public sector primary budget surplus of BRR8.97 billion. \par \par \uc2  The government has agreed with the IMF to post an accumulated surplus of BRR25 billion by June and BRR34.1 billion by September's end, when the current accord between the government and the IMF closes. \par \par \uc2  The government marginally beat the IMF target of BRR11.4 billion, posting a first quarter surplus of BRR11.555 billion. \par \par \uc2  But the April consolidated primary budget surplus numbers will likely dispel last month's fears. The government has to post a BRR4.5 billion surplus a month to meet its second quarter goal. April's result effectively takes care of two months in one go. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Dias said the government expects the Brazilian economy to grow 2% to 2.5% this year, which is in line with world growth and better than any other country in South America. \par \par \uc2  -By Adriana Arai, Anthony Dovkants, Gerald Jeffris and Terry Wade; Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1478; brazil@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020613dy6d000z6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
