\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Real Opens At BRR2.637/Dlr Vs BRR2.609 End Wed \b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2002\par 14:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones Energy Service \par \uc2 NRG\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Brazil's real continued to lose  ground at the open Thursday, as the country's debt selloff of late keeps on amplifying pre-election jitters. \par \par \uc2  The real opened at BRR2.637 to the dollar and was changing hands at BRR2.643 around 1300 GMT. Wednesday's close, the lowest in seven months for the second day running, was BRR2.609 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Brazil's Capitalization Bond was 2.94 lower  at 67.25, according to quotes provided by local financial  service Broadcast. \par \par \uc2  The recent downturn in domestic markets and growing worries that the government might have trouble rolling over its burdensome public debt are also paving the way for speculation, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Market players, unhappy about a recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure tightening accounting rules for fixed-income mutual funds, are taking the opportunity to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tolerance for a sinking \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There's people speculating against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a Sao Paulo-based trader said. \par \par \uc2  The real has shed more than 10 centavos - or 5% of its  value - since May 31, the day when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  enacted the new rules demanding funds to register debt price swings on a daily basis. \par \par \uc2  The new accounting rules for funds has dramatically weakened demand for treasury debt, as political jitters ahead of the October presidential election and an oversupply of bills have been adding unprecedented volatility to debt prices. \par \par \uc2  To calm markets, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week agreed to swap medium- and long-term government bills held by the market for shorter bonds that mature before President Fernando Henrique Cardoso leaves office at the end of the year. He can't seek a third mandate. \par \par \uc2  While the move initially helped ease tension, now analysts are worried about the country's ability to refund  domestic debt, one-third of which is maturing in the short run. \par \par \uc2  Yet investors selling reals and buying dollars at the current price level are betting on an extremely bearish scenario, traders said. Assuming an average interest rate of 17% this year, the real would have to weaken to about BRR2.90 to the dollar by the end of the year for them to make a profit, one bank director said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2   on the market, selling or buying dollars, if there's extreme volatility or low liquidity that could make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  decouple from the country's economic fundamentals. It did so last year to ward pressure stemming from the fallout of the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. and Argentine crisis contagion. \par \par \uc2  -By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; (5511) 3145-1481; adriana.arai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument nrg0000020020606dy66005v5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
