\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Fraga Says Turbulence On Funds Temporary \b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2002\par 22:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- Market turbulence surrounding changes in fixed-income accounting rules for local investment funds is a temporary phenomenon, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Arminio Fraga said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Over time, the market should return to normality," Fraga said, testifying before a congressional committee. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's investment-fund industry was thrown into momentary shock last week when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  advanced the deadline for funds to adjust the value of their fixed-income holdings to market rates on a daily basis. \par \par \uc2  According to some estimates, the market lost some 4 billion reals ($1=BRR2.59) overnight as funds reduced their asset values by about 1% on average. \par \par \uc2  The market havoc has coincided with a heavy schedule of dollar-linked debt coming due as a consequence of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year during the unfolding of an Argentine economic crisis and uncertainties following the Sept. 11 attacks in New York. \par \par \uc2  Fraga, meanwhile, Tuesday also gave reassurances that the country's Treasury wouldn't increase its exchange risk exposure with recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt-swap \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He explained the bank decided to roll over only the principal of debt coming due and not the interest. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts have shown concern about Brazil's exchanged-linked debt load amid recent pressure on the country's exchange rate. Brazil's real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has devalued more than 12% against the dollar since early April. \par \par \uc2  The country has offered the exchange swaps in tandem with LFT floating rate bills as a way to encourage investors to purchase more government debt. \par \par \uc2  Investors in Brazil, however, have been reluctant to take on new domestic debt ahead of the Oct. 6 presidential elections. The government on Tuesday sold just half of the BRR1.5 billion batch of January 2003 LTN fixed-rate treasury bills on offer at a rate of 19.45%. Last week, the government sold December 2002 bills at an average rate of 18.91%. \par \par \uc2  -By Gerald Jeffris, Dow Jones Newswires; (5561) 9965-6883; gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020604dy640015n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
