\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Brazil's CenBank sells dollars for 100th time. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniela Machado \par \uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2001\par 21:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Brazil, Nov 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  racked up on Tuesday its 100th \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since it began the controversial policy in July, but experts said the daily dollar sales may be rendered unnecessary. \par \par \uc2  In an attempt to inject more reais into Brazil's financial system and buoy what then appeared to be a sinking \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced on July 5 that it would spend up to $6 billion by year-end in the exchange market, selling about $50 million per day. \par \par \uc2  At 2.471 per dollar on Tuesday, the real is still 21 percent weaker than where it began 2001, but it is far off the 2.83 reais to which it plunged soon after the Sept. 11 attacks on the United States roiled markets worldwide. \par \par \uc2  While analysts say the daily sale of dollars has indeed helped steady the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many argue that it has also played only a supporting role in the real's recent stabilization. \par \par \uc2  "In my opinion, they don't need to continue now because the market has reached a point of maturity, but I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue nonetheless," said Emilio Garofalo, the bank's former chief for international reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "This team has a certain style; they prefer clarity and following through with what they've announced." \par \par \uc2  When asked about the possibility of discontinuing the daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank's director of monetary policy Luiz Fernando Figueiredo said: "That will happen after an announcement that will be made one day. \par \par \uc2  "What we need to remember is that our policy is that the less we need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the better." \par \par \uc2  Many experts say the daily sales should at least continue until the bank finishes spending the $6 billion. Since July, it has spent about $5.12 billion. \par \par \uc2  PART OF LARGER ARSENAL \par \par \uc2  The real was hit repeatedly this year by tensions in Argentina, an electricity shortage and political corruption scandals that weakened investors' confidence in the country. \par \par \uc2  Worries of a global economic slowdown that would quash investment in Brazil was only exacerbated by the Sept. 11 attacks. \par \par \uc2  But Brazil's economy appears to have been more resilient than initially expected despite the global slowdown, and investors have largely stopped selling Brazilian assets solely because of the country's ties to troubled Argentina. \par \par \uc2  Analysts admit the daily dollar sales helped steady the real but add that the program was part of a larger arsenal of measures meant to keep investors from buying dollars. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to depreciate through July, August and September, critics of the policy said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was being too transparent and had lost its advantage of surprising the market with its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The $50 million per day are part of the story. They were a group of measures, and it is difficult to say what role the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  played alone," said Antonio Correa de Lacerda, the president of the Brazilian Society for the Study of Transnational Companies (Sobeet). \par \par \uc2  By the end of September, the bank had tightened liquidity by raising reserve requirements on banks and the cost of their exposure to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At the same time, it has sold dollar-linked notes that were sometimes more popular with investors than dollars themselves. \par \par \uc2  "The objective of the dollar sales was very clear. It wasn't to steady the dollar but to reduce volatility in the market, which would have caused problems for exporters and importers," said William Eid Junior, a professor at Sao Paulo's Fundacao Getulio Vargas. \par \par \uc2  PSYCHOLOGICAL EFFECT \par \par \uc2  Garofalo believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could even use reverse psychology on the market if it decided to suspend the daily sales before the $6 billion was spent. He said such a move could have the opposite effect to what happened when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates at the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  "At that point, the feeling in the market was that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was lending credence to a pessimistic outlook," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Now, markets might say, 'The scenario is better and, if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspending the daily sales, it's because it must be true.'". \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011127dxbr03jmo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
